## First tasks

1. **Data.** `src/lib/sample.ts` holds example data that ships with the
   template. Replace it with the app's real data or its loader before showing
   the app to anyone, and never present example figures as real ones.
[%- if kind == 'monitor' %]
   For live state, `useLiveState(url, example)` in `src/lib/live.ts` reads
   server-sent events from a service such as a lathe service project, which
   sends the current state on connect and each new state after it. Pass its
   `status` to the screen's `status` prop, and show skeletons from
   `src/blocks/states.tsx` while the state is still null.
[%- endif %]
2. **Icon.** Design an icon for this app and put it in both
   `src/app-icon.tsx` (the app bar) and `public/favicon.svg` (the browser
   tab); both hold an initials placeholder now. Make it a simple geometric
   mark tied to what the app does, legible at 16 px, on the accent colour.
   The component uses `var(--primary)` so it follows the theme; the favicon
   cannot read page styles, so it takes the accent as a hex value.
3. **Name and copy.** The screen's labels are examples too. Rename sections
   and write labels in the users' own terms.

## Commands

```bash
npm run dev          # hot reload
npm run fix          # format and apply safe lint fixes
npx lefthook run pre-commit --all-files   # every commit gate
npx lefthook run pre-push --all-files     # build, screenshots[% if artifact %], artifact[% endif %]
```

`npm run check` writes screenshots to `checks/` (light and dark; desktop,
laptop and phone). It fails on broken charts, spilling panels, sideways
scrolling and chart labels that collide or overrun their panel. Look at the
screenshots after every visual change; the check catches breakage, not ugly
layouts. Before the app is shown or published,
offer the user reviews by Claude Code's `ui-critic` agent (visual polish) and
`hig-critic` agent (Apple's guidelines: purpose, legibility, states and
writing). Expect them to disagree sometimes, and settle those points with the
user.

## Rules

- Colours come from theme tokens only (`bg-card`, `text-ink-2`,
  `bg-primary-soft`, `text-good`, `var(--series-1)` and so on). Never write a
  hex value or a Tailwind palette colour such as `blue-500` in a component.
- Charts go through `PlotFigure` in `src/blocks/plot-figure.tsx`, which reads
  colours from the theme. Assign series slots in order and never cycle past the
  last one; fold extra series into "Other" or facet instead.
- Build from `src/blocks` and `src/components/ui` before writing new
  components. Add shadcn components with `npx shadcn add <name>`.
- Status always pairs a colour with an icon and a word (`StatusBadge`).
- Legibility over compliance: these are personal tools, so accessibility
  compliance is not a goal, but reading must be effortless. Text at least
  11px (13px for running text), about 4.5:1 contrast for text the user reads
  (`text-faint` is for decoration and large figures), controls comfortable to
  hit on phone layouts, keyboard paths for frequent actions, and status never
  carried by colour alone.
- Every view has its states: loading shows something at once, empty states
  invite the next action, and errors say what happened and how to fix it.
- Labels say what happens ("Release volume", not "Submit"), an action keeps
  its name through the flow, and capitalization is sentence case throughout.
- Change the direction only through `theme` in `app.config.json`
  (`instrument`, `editorial` or `console`); never edit `theme/`, which
  `copier update` replaces.
- Keep every gate green, and never weaken one to get there.
- Commit messages never credit an AI assistant as an author; a hook rejects
  them. Stage explicit paths, never `git add -A` or `git add .`.
